Author: Souheil Ben Ayed <souheil@tera.ics.keio.ac.jp>



----- SUMMARY -----

DiamEAP is an implementation of the Diameter Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP) Application (RFC 4072).
The Diameter protocol is a AAA protocol to securely carry Authentication, Authorization and Accounting information between the AAA client and the AAA server.
Diameter EAP Application is a Diameter application that supports authentication using Extensible Authentication Protocol (RFC 3748).  

DiamEAP is designed to be extensible so that any new EAP method can be implemented separately as a shared library called 'EAP method plug-in'.
Currently, DiamEAP comprises two EAP Methods plug-ins: 
	- EAP MD5 plug-in : implementation of EAP-MD5 authentication method.
	- EAP TLS plug-in : implementation of EAP-TLS authentication method .

Other EAP methods are scheduled to be added in future releases. 	
	
DiamEAP is an extension for freeDiameter. Visit http://www.freediameter.net/ for more information on freeDiameter project.

For more information on DiamEAP project, visit http://diameap.yagami.freediameter.net/



----- REQUIREMENTS -----

The extension requires the MySQL client development library, Gcrypt, and
GnuTLS. It is built as part of freeDiameter; the project build also requires
its normal CMake, Flex, and Bison prerequisites. Consult the platform-specific
freeDiameter installation notes for package names.


----- BUILDING -----

From the freeDiameter source tree, configure an out-of-tree build with
DiamEAP enabled:

    mkdir test.diameap
    cd test.diameap
    cmake .. -DBUILD_APP_DIAMEAP=ON
    cmake --build .

The extension is disabled by default. The EAP Identity method plug-in is
enabled by default when DiamEAP is built; EAP-MD5 and EAP-TLS are optional
(`BUILD_EAP_MD5` and `BUILD_EAP_TLS`). EAP-TLS also links against GnuTLS.
Built extensions use the `.fdx` suffix and EAP method plug-ins use `.emp`.

Create a MySQL database and initialize it using
`extensions/app_diameap/diameap.sql` before starting the extension.


----- CONFIGURATION -----

Start with [`doc/app_diameap.conf.sample`](../../doc/app_diameap.conf.sample).
It includes the supported directives and a minimal EAP Identity plug-in
example. Set the `DiamEAP_MySQL` directive to the credentials, server, and
database created for this installation. The SQL schema is
`extensions/app_diameap/diameap.sql`.

The EAP-TLS configuration example is
[`doc/eap_tls_plugin.diameap.conf.sample`](../../doc/eap_tls_plugin.diameap.conf.sample).
Copy it to a runtime configuration file and supply certificate, key, and CA
paths as needed. In the DiamEAP configuration, use paths that resolve from the
daemon's working directory or absolute paths; for example, the sample uses
`extensions/eap_identity.emp` for the identity plug-in. Update these paths to
match the actual build/install layout. Load the built `app_diameap.fdx` in the
main freeDiameter configuration.

Authorization is disabled by default and can be enabled with
`Authorization = 1;`. The default multi-round timeout is 30 seconds and the
default maximum number of invalid EAP packets is 5; the sample shows the
directives for changing these values.


For more documentation on configuring DiamEAP, visit the documentation page of the project : http://diameap.yagami.freediameter.net/documentation



